  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There is a marginal risk of excessive \nrainfall for Virginia and the Carolinas today/tonight. \n\nA very moist airmass remains in place today with persistent\neasterly flow advecting deep moisture into the region. Surface\nanalysis indicates that a stalled boundary is draped over the\nregion, in addition to a wave of low pressure that is easily \nidentifiable in the satellite water vapor imagery. The \nconcerning factor is the low is nearly stationary which means \nsteering winds will remain stagnant with little or no geographic\nchange in the position of the surface front today. As such, \nexpect persistent formation of showers and a few storms within \nthe vicinity of the front, with heaviest of the rain today \nfalling over southern Virginia and into North Carolina. This is \nwhere hi-res CAM guidance continues to pinpoint on the greatest \ncoverage of heavy rainfall.\n\n12z RNK RAOB shows an increase in Precipitable Water when \ncompared to yesterday and current RAP analysis continues to \nincrease these values, with some of the area now closing in on\n2 inch values. This will result in very efficient rainfall \nwhich could lead to an increased potential for flash flooding \nthrough late this evening.\n\nAway from the convergent zone near the surface front, not \nexpecting a lot of shower coverage. However, heating of the day\ninstability driven showers are still possible but they should \nnot be as organized as what is expected along the actual surface\nfront. The Storm Prediction Center has highlighted a small\nmarginal risk area for strong to severe storms over western\nNorth Carolina...an area closer to the center of the upper low.\nWarming surface temperatures in the higher terrain will result \nin scattered convective initiation across the southern \nAppalachians. As instability peaks and low-level lapse rates \nbecome steep over the next few hours, an isolated wind-damage \nthreat is expected to develop in the western Carolinas.\n\nBy tonight, loss of daytime heating should mitigate any severe\nthreat, but a localized excessive rain hazard will linger. The \nsurface front over southern VA should begin to lift north as the\nupper low begins to open up and shear out. This should\nreconfigure the axis of low level convergence with areas closer\nto the US 460/I-64 corridor targeted for the higher qpf \novernight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather will persist through the rest\nof this week with daily chances of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nThe upper low over the Mid-Atlantic will shear out and weaken\nWednesday. Meanwhile a large upper level ridge builds over \nBermuda and another upper level trough approaches the Great \nLakes. Wind flow between these two features will maintain a\npersistent flow of moisture into the forecast area, enough to\nfuel daily showers and thunderstorms, most of which should\ncoincide with the peak heating of the day favoring the afternoon\nand evening hours. We are not expecting anything severe, but a\nmarginal risk for flash flooding will continue to be a hazard\ndue to the potential for high rain rates which may lead to\nrunoff issues.",
